WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.720
It’s not the best time to&nbsp;
be a Russian sausage tycoon.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.820
A Russian military general.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.220
The editor of a Russian tabloid.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.200
The head of a Russian ski resort.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.240
A Russian rail magnate.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.280
A Russian shipyard magnate.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.700
Or Russian oil and gas executives.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:24.060
On Christmas Eve, Pavel Antov reportedly fell&nbsp;
from the third-floor window of his hotel in India.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.940
He had just celebrated his 65th birthday.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.180
Besides his success in the&nbsp;
meat processing industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.480
Antov was a member of a&nbsp;
regional Russian parliament.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.220
He appeared to criticize a Russian missile&nbsp;
attack in a residential district in Kyiv.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.620
A message from his WhatsApp account read:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.700
"It's extremely difficult to call&nbsp;
all this anything but terror."

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.100
He later claimed that the message was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.360
someone else’s - not his - and&nbsp;
reaffirmed his support of Putin.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.520
So many Russian business executives&nbsp;
have mysteriously died that there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.040
an entire Wikipedia page dedicated to them.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.480
The number of dead reached 25 by the end of 2022.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.820
Oil executive Aleksandr Subbotin&nbsp;
was allegedly poisoned with toad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.200
venom in the home of a “shaman”. 
 
To better understand what’s going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.780
I spoke with an authority figure on Russia.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.480
So I wanted to ask you if you believe that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.020
Kremlin is behind them all or if&nbsp;
the answer is more complicated?

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.720
It’s surprisingly enough more complicated. To&nbsp;
be honest, look, the Kremlin on the whole does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.240
not need to kill people. The Kremlin has so many&nbsp;
other ways that it can threaten and intimidate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.900
and if need be, imprison. 
 
Mark Galeotti is a professor at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.400
University College London who recently&nbsp;
released his book: Putin’s Wars.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.540
What we’re seeing is in part genuine cases&nbsp;
of accidents and suicides, particularly for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:55.620
people who have suddenly lost their lifestyles&nbsp;
or business empires and so forth. But also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:01.740
a resurgence of something we saw in the 1990s,&nbsp;
which is in some ways murder as a business tactic.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.700
Russia was in political and economic turmoil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.540
following the collapse of&nbsp;
the Soviet Union in 1991.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.480
Violence as a way of doing business was the norm.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.120
What we did see was a lot of clashes,&nbsp;
rivalries over suddenly available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.280
markets and so forth. And at that&nbsp;
time, absolutely, we saw a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.027
spate of killings ranging from opportunistic,&nbsp;
someone being shot in a car park, all the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.027 --> 00:02:24.120
through to some incredibly sophisticated ones&nbsp;
involving radioactive materials and the like.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:30.420
Vladimir Putin was credited as the leader who&nbsp;
restored stability in the 2000s and 2010s.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.180
However, following Russia’s invasion of&nbsp;
Ukraine and the crush of Western sanctions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.500
Galeotti believes the cutthroat&nbsp;
way of doing business is back.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:46.320
All of a sudden, shall we say, the cake is&nbsp;
shrinking at quite a dramatic pace. And therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.480
this is what’s happening: we’re seeing&nbsp;
the resurgence of a lot of battle waves in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.620
which actually, all of a sudden, people&nbsp;
are competing that much more fiercely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.180
for controlling companies, controlling&nbsp;
resources, controls of markets.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.380
I don’t think this is a kind of&nbsp;
sinister plot by the Kremlin.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.800
Another Russian expert I spoke&nbsp;
with came to the same conclusion. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.840
Peter Rutland is a professor of government&nbsp;
at Wesleyan University in Connecticut.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:19.980
The sanctions that the West imposed on Russia;&nbsp;
the freezing of bank assets, the seizure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.320
some foreign assets, over 1,000 individuals&nbsp;
have been put on sanctions lists by Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:31.620
governments. So that’s made the lives of Russian&nbsp;
businesspeople very difficult, very stressful.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.880
In a country with the third highest&nbsp;
suicide mortality rate in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.620
he says it's highly probable that some&nbsp;
of the tycoons took their own lives.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.700
To be clear, the Kremlin&nbsp;
does kill when it wants to.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:52.080
In 2006, Alexander Litvinenko, a former Russian&nbsp;
intelligence agent who defected, was poisoned with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.940
a cup of tea containing the radioactive&nbsp;
compound polonium at a hotel in London.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.660
Russia is the only country that produces polonium.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.500
The conclusion that the Russian&nbsp;
state was probably involved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.860
the murder of Mr. Litvinenko is deeply disturbing,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.840
Litvinenko’s story has now been&nbsp;
dramatized in a mini-series.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.600
The man who says I must die, I know this person.&nbsp;

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.100
What is his name?
His name is Vladimir Putin.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.660
There’s been a long history of&nbsp;
poisonings linked to the Kremlin.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:30.480
Jailed opposition leader, Alexei Navalny&nbsp;
was nearly killed by a nerve agent in 2020.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:36.480
Investigative journalist Anna Politkovskaya, a&nbsp;
vocal Putin critic, was poisoned on a flight but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.240
survived. Two years later, she was shot and killed&nbsp;
in her apartment elevator on Putin’s birthday.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:49.080
However, these political targets are unlike the&nbsp;
recent deaths involving corporate executives.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.800
Businesspeople, frankly, you know,&nbsp;
if they want to get rid of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.500
they can just arrest them. It’s a lot easier.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.100
So, as easy as it may seem to&nbsp;
point the finger at the Kremlin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.680
the bigger picture is more complicated.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.160
Nonetheless, the deaths are very suspicious.&nbsp;
Some even involve entire families.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.980
Sergey Protosenya, the former deputy head&nbsp;
of Russia’s second-largest gas company, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.960
accused of killing his wife and daughter at their&nbsp;
Spanish villa with an axe, before hanging himself.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.900
However, his former employer, Novatek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.920
said speculations about his death&nbsp;
“bear no relation to reality”.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.060
This was backed up by Sergey’s son,&nbsp;
who was not in Spain at the time.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:36.360
He told the UK’s Daily Mail: 'He loved my&nbsp;
mother and especially Maria my sister. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.380
was his princess. He could never&nbsp;
do anything to harm them. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.520
know what happened that night, but I&nbsp;
know that my dad did not hurt them.'

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.220
If the death toll continues in 2023,&nbsp;
speculation over their cause will remain strong.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:54.480
As former Soviet Defence&nbsp;
Minister Georgy Zhukov once said:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.080
“There are things in Russia&nbsp;
which are not as they seem.”

